README for nxpwifiutl
=====================

Overview
--------
`nxpwifiutl` is a userspace utility designed to interact with the NXP Wi-Fi driver (`nxpwifi`) via the `nl80211` netlink interface. It supports various vendor-specific commands for configuring and debugging advanced features of NXP IW61x chipsets, such as host sleep, channel switching, antenna configuration, EDMAC, VHT, CSI, clock synchronization, and independent reset.

Environment
-----------
- Compatible with Linux kernel versions 6.6.52.
- Requires nxpwifi.ko and nxpwifi_sdio.ko driver to be loaded.
- Interface names can be mlan0, uap0.

Usage
-----
    nxpwifiutl <ifname> <cmd> [args...]

- `ifname`: Wireless interface name, e.g., `mlan0`, `uap0`
- `cmd`: One of the supported commands listed below
- `args`: Optional arguments depending on the command

Supported Commands
------------------
| Command         | Description |
|----------------|-------------|
| hscfg          | Configure host sleep parameters (conditions, GPIO, gap). |
| sleeppd        | Configure sleep period for PPS/uAPSD. |
| hsoffload      | Enable/disable auto ARP, Ping, Wake-on-mDNS offload. |
| channel_switch | Configure channel switch parameters (oper class, channel, bandwidth). |
| antcfg         | Configure antenna mode and SAD evaluation time. |
| edmac_cfg      | Configure Energy Detect MAC (2G/5G offsets, txq lock). |
| vhtcfg         | Configure VHT (802.11ac) IE (band, txrx, bw, capabilities, MCS map). |
| csi            | Configure CSI (Channel State Information) collection. |
| clocksync      | Configure GPIO TSF latch for clock synchronization. |
| indrstcfg      | Configure independent reset mode and GPIO pin. |
| hostcmd        | Send raw hostcmd from config file (e.g., edmac_ctrl, txpwrlimit). |

Examples
--------
    # Query host sleep configuration
    nxpwifiutl mlan0 hscfg

    # Set host sleep parameters
    nxpwifiutl mlan0 hscfg 0x7 5 200

    # Configure channel switch
    nxpwifiutl uap0 channel_switch 1 115 36 10 4 3

Notes
-----
- Most commands support both query and set modes: if only `<cmd>` is provided, it performs a query; if additional arguments are given, it performs a configuration.
- The `hostcmd` command requires a config file, for example:
      nxpwifiutl mlan0 hostcmd txpwrlimit_cfg.conf txpwrlimit_2g_cfg_set
```

